						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">I&#039;ve lived my entire life on the Gulf Coast.  Every summer and fall, the local weather constantly surveys the Atlantic Ocean, the Carribean Sea and the Gulf of Mexico watching for low pressures that can develop into tropical depressions and later on, possibly, into hurricanes.  It&#039;s part of the risk of living in this region.  Everyone knows that it&#039;s not a question of if, but WHEN, a major hurricane will come through and wreak havoc.  Every single city on the Gulf Coast has horror stories about past hurricanes, and they know damn well what can happen when one comes through.<br /><br />Everyone is taught early on to evacuate when a hurricane is coming.  They usually have days, as New Orleans did for Katrina, to prepare.  Everyone pretty much boards up their windows and prepares for the worst and LEAVES.  <br /><br />Why do they evacuate?  Because they know that hurricanes can wreak havoc.  Flooding, no power for days, sometimes weeks, major wind damage.....all of this happens every, single time.<br /><br />Everyone....and I mean everyone, even the idiots who didn&#039;t evacuate New Orleans, know about this.  <br /><br />To me, the set of people that had the most capability to save those people, were those people themselves.  Sure, it would have been nice to get instant help for hundreds of thousands of people, but folks, this was a major natural disaster.  You&#039;re not going to get everything you want in major natural disasters.  I know that if I had elected to ride out a class 5 hurricane, I would not assume that I was going to live through it, and I certainly would expect to see my house and property severely damaged.  <br /><br />I don&#039;t like it when people try to blame others for problems that they bought onto themsleves, and the people who refused to evacuate New Orleans are doing just that.  <br /><br /></td>
